    "result": "`search_products` is implemented in `solution.py`, and all 14 tests in `tests/test_solution.py` pass with `python -m pytest -q tests`.\n\nIt builds one parameterized query that filters on `stock > 0`, an optional `price <= max_price`, and a substring match using `LIKE ... ESCAPE`. `%`, `_` and `\\` in the search text are escaped, so they match literally, and `LIKE` is already case-insensitive for ASCII. Results are ordered by `name` or `price`, in ascending or descending order, with `id` ascending as the tie-breaker either way. `LIMIT` and `OFFSET` are applied last, and each row comes back as `{id, name, price}`. It raises `ValueError` for a bad `order_by`, a `limit` outside 1–100, or a negative `offset`; booleans and non-integers are rejected for both `limit` and `offset`. The query is a read-only `SELECT`.\n\nThe tests cover the stock filter, case-insensitivity, literal `%` and `_`, the price cap, tie-breaking in both directions, binary name ordering, paging and the invalid-argument cases. Two of my own expected orderings were wrong on the first runs. I fixed the test expectations, not the code.",
